<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Australopithecus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as australopithecines, australopiths</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Australopithecus is an extinct genus of early hominins that lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> during the Pliocene and Early Pleistocene, from some of whose species the genera Homo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paranthropus">Paranthropus</a> and Kenyanthropus evolved. Its earliest known member, A. anamensis, lived in eastern Africa around 4.2 million years ago, and the genus became extinct 1.9 million years ago. Its species, among them A. afarensis, A. africanus and A. sediba, combined a small brain with developed bipedality, which upset the theory that large brain size had preceded bipedalism. The first specimen, the skull of the Taung Child, was found in 1924 at Taung, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and named Australopithecus africanus by Raymond Dart.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/australopithecus.jpg" alt="Australopithecus" width="330" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Braga; Didier Descouens · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mrs_Ples_Face.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At Hadar, Donald Johanson and Tom Gray found the partial skeleton of a 3.2-million-year-old <b>Australopithecus</b> afarensis.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-24">24 November in history</a></li></ul></section>
